          "model_selection_plan": "Selected 3 models from the candidate pool based on EDA findings on the bank marketing dataset (binary classification, target='y'):\n\n1. **LogisticRegression** — Selected as the interpretable baseline. The dataset has 36K rows with mixed numerical/categorical features. Logistic regression provides coefficient interpretability (important for marketing insights) and serves as a strong linear benchmark. Requires one-hot encoding and scaling, which we'll apply. Class imbalance (~88/12) will be handled via class_weight='balanced'.\n\n2. **RandomForest** — Selected as the robust ensemble method. Handles non-linear relationships and feature interactions naturally (e.g., job × month, age × balance). No scaling needed, label encoding suffices for categoricals. The 'duration' leakage concern is less severe here as trees can isolate it. Outliers in pdays/previous are handled by tree-based splits. Class imbalance handled via class_weight='balanced_subsample'.\n\n3. **XGBoost** — Selected as the high-performance gradient boosting model. Typically outperforms RandomForest on structured tabular data like this. Supports scale_pos_weight for class imbalance. Handles missing values (none here) and mixed data types well. Early stopping prevents overfitting. Likely to achieve the highest ROC-AUC among the three.\n\n**Why not SVM?** SVM with RBF kernel requires extensive hyperparameter tuning, doesn't scale well to 36K rows with one-hot encoding (high dimensionality), and is sensitive to imbalanced data. It would be computationally expensive with limited performance gain over XGBoost.\n\n**Why not KNN?** KNN is distance-based and suffers from the curse of dimensionality with 12+ one-hot encoded categorical columns. It also requires scaling and performs poorly on imbalanced data where the majority class dominates nearest neighbors."
